{"causes": ["The earlier Khmer capital of Hariharalaya, established by Indravarman I, faced spatial limitations for a rapidly expanding empire.", "Indravarman I's successful consolidation of power and expansion of the empire provided a stable foundation for his successor.", "The established tradition of the Devaraja cult, initiated by Jayavarman II, necessitated a grand, new capital to house the state god-king's temple."], "effects": ["The establishment of Yasodharapura as the enduring capital provided the foundational urban plan for the subsequent Angkor Thom.", "The construction of the East Baray initiated a period of large-scale hydraulic engineering critical for the Angkorian agricultural economy.", "Yasovarman I's reign fostered a cultural and architectural flourishing that would be continued and expanded by later Khmer monarchs."], "relationships": [{"sourceSlug": "indravarman-i", "sourceName": "Indravarman I", "verb": "INFLUENCES", "targetSlug": "yasovarman-i", "targetName": "Yasovarman I", "context": "Indravarman I, Yasovarman I's father, laid the groundwork for centralized power and initial building projects that influenced his son's reign."}, {"sourceSlug": "yasovarman-i", "sourceName": "Yasovarman I", "verb": "CREATES", "targetSlug": "angkor-thom", "targetName": "Angkor Thom", "context": "Yasovarman I founded the city of Yasodharapura in 889 CE, which served as the blueprint and direct predecessor for the later Angkor Thom."}, {"sourceSlug": "yasovarman-i", "sourceName": "Yasovarman I", "verb": "OCCURS_IN", "targetSlug": "angkor-cambodia", "targetName": "Angkor, Cambodia", "context": "The reign of Yasovarman I was entirely centered within the Angkor region, establishing its enduring capital there."}, {"sourceSlug": "jayavarman-iv", "sourceName": "Jayavarman IV", "verb": "SUCCEEDS", "targetSlug": "yasovarman-i", "targetName": "Yasovarman I", "context": "Jayavarman IV eventually succeeded Yasovarman I, though not directly, following a period of political instability after Yasovarman's death in 910 CE."}, {"sourceSlug": "yasovarman-i", "sourceName": "Yasovarman I", "verb": "TRANSFORMS", "targetSlug": "khmer-empire", "targetName": "Khmer Empire", "context": "Yasovarman I transformed the Khmer Empire by relocating its capital and establishing a new urban and hydraulic paradigm that defined its classical period."}], "places": [{"name": "Angkor, Cambodia", "role": "Capital region"}, {"name": "Yasodharapura, Cambodia", "role": "Founded capital"}, {"name": "Phnom Bakheng, Cambodia", "role": "State temple site"}], "texts": [{"title": "Wikipedia: Yasovarman I", "type": "Reference article", "slug": "yasovarman-i"}], "externalLinks": ["https://www.wikidata.org/wiki/Q559810", "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Yasovarman_I"], "tags": ["Monarchs & Rulers", "political-systems", "empire-and-colonialism", "geopolitical-linkage"], "thumbnailUrl": "", "quote": "", "legacySummary": "", "historicalSignificance": {"significanceScore": 7, "significanceNarrative": "Yasovarman I's decision to establish Yasodharapura (Angkor Thom) and construct the East Baray fundamentally shaped the urban and agricultural core of the Khmer Empire for centuries, directly enabling its monumental building projects and sustaining its population.", "significanceCategory": "regional"}, "_editLog": [{"timestamp": "2026-06-29T07:59:51Z", "editorId": "gemini-2.5-flash·cloud·GH#28357169009", "field": "summary", "oldValue": "Yasovarman I was an Angkorian king who reigned in 889–910 CE. He was called "Leper King".", "newValue": "Yasovarman I, reigning from 889 to 910 CE, was a pivotal Angkorian monarch whose reign fundamentally reshaped the Khmer Empire's political and urban landscape. Often remembered as the 'Leper King' due to a posthumous epithet, his true legacy lies in the strategic relocation of the capital to Yasodharapura, the precursor to Angkor Thom, establishing a new administrative and religious center that would endure for centuries. His rule marked a period of consolidating the Devaraja cult and expanding the empire's hydraulic infrastructure, critical for agricultural prosperity and the projection of royal power.

His most significant achievement was the founding of Yasodharapura in 889 CE, strategically positioned around Phnom Bakheng, which served as his state temple. This new capital, located north of the earlier Hariharalaya, was designed to be grander and more centrally located within the expanding agricultural plains. Yasovarman I oversaw the construction of significant monuments, including the Lolei temple (dedicated to his ancestors) and the massive East Baray, an artificial reservoir measuring approximately 7.5 by 1.8 kilometers, crucial for water management and symbolizing royal command over nature. His reign also saw the establishment of numerous ashramas (hermitages) for different religious sects, reflecting a policy of religious tolerance.

The urban plan and sophisticated hydraulic engineering initiated by Yasovarman I provided the foundational template for subsequent Angkorian kings, influencing the layout and development of the entire Angkor complex. The East Baray, though later supplemented by the West Baray, remained a testament to his foresight in managing water resources for a burgeoning population. His architectural innovations and the strategic choice of capital profoundly shaped the Khmer Empire's continued growth, allowing for the vast agricultural surpluses that fueled its monumental building programs."}, {"timestamp": "2026-06-29T07:59:51Z", "editorId": "gemini-2.5-flash·cloud·GH#28357169009", "field": "detailsJson.causes", "oldValue": [{"title": "Medieval era: interplay of religious authority, feudal governance, and trade network expansion", "type": "EventWindow", "year": "900 CE"}, {"title": "Dynastic succession and sovereign authority traditions", "type": "Idea", "year": "900 CE"}], "newValue": ["The earlier Khmer capital of Hariharalaya, established by Indravarman I, faced spatial limitations for a rapidly expanding empire.", "Indravarman I's successful consolidation of power and expansion of the empire provided a stable foundation for his successor.", "The established tradition of the Devaraja cult, initiated by Jayavarman II, necessitated a grand, new capital to house the state god-king's temple."]}, {"timestamp": "2026-06-29T07:59:51Z", "editorId": "gemini-2.5-flash·cloud·GH#28357169009", "field": "detailsJson.effects", "oldValue": [{"title": "Established dynastic legacies and territorial boundaries", "type": "Idea", "year": "910 CE"}, {"title": "Contributed to synthesis of classical and religious thought informing Renaissance and Reformati", "type": "Movement", "year": "910 CE"}], "newValue": ["The establishment of Yasodharapura as the enduring capital provided the foundational urban plan for the subsequent Angkor Thom.", "The construction of the East Baray initiated a period of large-scale hydraulic engineering critical for the Angkorian agricultural economy.", "Yasovarman I's reign fostered a cultural and architectural flourishing that would be continued and expanded by later Khmer monarchs."]}, {"timestamp": "2026-06-29T07:59:51Z", "editorId": "gemini-2.5-flash·cloud·GH#28357169009", "field": "detailsJson.relationships", "oldValue": [{"sourceSlug": "yasovarman-i", "sourceName": "Yasovarman I", "verb": "RULES", "targetSlug": "field-221", "targetName": "Monarchs & Rulers", "context": "Yasovarman I: Sovereign authority in monarchs & rulers"}, {"sourceSlug": "yasovarman-i", "sourceName": "Yasovarman I", "verb": "OCCURS_DURING", "targetSlug": "medieval", "targetName": "Medieval Era", "context": "Yasovarman I was active during the Medieval era"}], "newValue": [{"sourceSlug": "indravarman-i", "sourceName": "Indravarman I", "verb": "INFLUENCES", "targetSlug": "yasovarman-i", "targetName": "Yasovarman I", "context": "Indravarman I, Yasovarman I's father, laid the groundwork for centralized power and initial building projects that influenced his son's reign."}, {"sourceSlug": "yasovarman-i", "sourceName": "Yasovarman I", "verb": "CREATES", "targetSlug": "angkor-thom", "targetName": "Angkor Thom", "context": "Yasovarman I founded the city of Yasodharapura in 889 CE, which served as the blueprint and direct predecessor for the later Angkor Thom."}, {"sourceSlug": "yasovarman-i", "sourceName": "Yasovarman I", "verb": "OCCURS_IN", "targetSlug": "angkor-cambodia", "targetName": "Angkor, Cambodia", "context": "The reign of Yasovarman I was entirely centered within the Angkor region, establishing its enduring capital there."}, {"sourceSlug": "jayavarman-iv", "sourceName": "Jayavarman IV", "verb": "SUCCEEDS", "targetSlug": "yasovarman-i", "targetName": "Yasovarman I", "context": "Jayavarman IV eventually succeeded Yasovarman I, though not directly, following a period of political instability after Yasovarman's death in 910 CE."}, {"sourceSlug": "yasovarman-i", "sourceName": "Yasovarman I", "verb": "TRANSFORMS", "targetSlug": "khmer-empire", "targetName": "Khmer Empire", "context": "Yasovarman I transformed the Khmer Empire by relocating its capital and establishing a new urban and hydraulic paradigm that defined its classical period."}]}, {"timestamp": "2026-06-29T07:59:51Z", "editorId": "gemini-2.5-flash·cloud·GH#28357169009", "field": "detailsJson.places", "oldValue": [], "newValue": [{"name": "Angkor, Cambodia", "role": "Capital region"}, {"name": "Yasodharapura, Cambodia", "role": "Founded capital"}, {"name": "Phnom Bakheng, Cambodia", "role": "State temple site"}]}, {"timestamp": "2026-06-29T07:59:51Z", "editorId": "gemini-2.5-flash·cloud·GH#28357169009", "field": "subjects", "oldValue": ["Monarchs & Rulers"], "newValue": ["Khmer Empire", "Southeast Asian History", "Ancient Architecture", "Hydraulic Engineering", "Urban Planning", "Monarchy", "Medieval Asia", "Religious Tolerance"]}, {"timestamp": "2026-06-29T07:59:51Z", "editorId": "gemini-2.5-flash·cloud·GH#28357169009", "field": "frameworks", "oldValue": ["POLITICAL_SYSTEMS", "EMPIRE_AND_COLONIALISM", "GEOPOLITICAL_LINKAGE", "TEMPORAL_LINKAGE"], "newValue": ["STRUCTURAL_ANALYSIS", "LONGUE_DUREE", "COMPARATIVE_CIVILIZATIONS"]}], "_unsyncedEdits": true}
